# G003 — Prime Factorization

*v2. See `prime-factors.md` for prose: factorization decomposes a whole
into irreducible parts, structurally analogous to choreography
projecting a global intention into agent-local slices.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Project Decomposition

[decompose-project @project
    IN ORDER TO
        break @project's complexity into irreducible factors
        assign narrative weight and resource cost per factor concurrently
        and verify the reassembled assignments still equal the whole

    @factors <- [prime-factors {n: @project:complexity-score}]

    concurrent [
        @sylvia{assign narrative weight to each of: @factors}
    |
        @kathryn{assign resource cost to each of: @factors}
    ]
    join

    <- @eliana{verify decomposition covers the whole}
        where no factor is left unassigned
        and product of assigned factors equals @project:complexity-score
]

---

## Verb Surface

[prime-factors
    n               {n}                       ;; -> [Int]
    factorization   {n}                        ;; -> {prime -> exponent}
    is-prime        {n}                        ;; -> Bool
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: fundamental-theorem `where` product reconstructs

[prime-factorization {n: @n}]
    returns a unique multiset of primes
    where product of primes (with multiplicity) equals @n
    IN ORDER TO
        encode the fundamental theorem of arithmetic as a constraint
        so "did the decomposition cover the whole?" has a yes/no answer
        applicable equally to integers and to project decompositions
]

Every irreducible factor is a concern; the choreography IS the
projection of a global intention into local slices. G003 is the
first project where *math structure mirrors coordination structure*.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [prime-factors {n: 12}]         ;; -> [2, 2, 3]
    -> [prime-factors {n: 60}]         ;; -> [2, 2, 3, 5]
    -> [prime-factorization {n: 60}]   ;; -> {2: 2, 3: 1, 5: 1}
    -> [prime-factors {n: 13}]         ;; -> [13] (prime)
]
